Short:  AC is the lower the better, including negative 
values.  (A)D&D relic.  

Long:  A long time ago some guys made an RPG called D&D (Dungeons 
and Dragons, later called AD&D, Advanced D&D).  It was a rather 
simple, Hack'n'Slash type of RPG, and was quickly supported 
with an abundance of supplements, expansions, campaign settings 
etc., boosting it to a quite detailed RPG with some fleshed-out 
campaign settings (most famous are Forgotten Realms/Dragonlance 
and Greyhawk).  Besides being the most important RPG for quite 
some time (and maybe even today), it heavily influenced many 
computer RPGs as well, and one can find in many games traces 
of D&D.  One example is the armour class, AC.  (A)D&D took an 
extremely simple combat system approach:  Each individual has 
an AC determined mainly by what armor he/she/it is wearing.  
Another individual trying to hit this guy had to make an attack 
roll; the to-hit probability depended on the attacker's level 
(and class, and some boni maybe) and the AC of the defender.  
And for some strange reason they counted AC backwards, beginning 
with 10 for unarmoured down to (IIRC) 0 for Full Plate+shield.  
But of course there were additional boni for AC possible, hence 
they expanded the AC range to the negative integers.  They 
fixed this now in the 3rd edition, but you'll find the old 
'inverse AC' system (and the whole D&D combat concept) in 
many games, e.g. in some of the Wizardry series (IIRC).  And 
also in nethack, although it uses different to-hit probabilities 
than AD&D.  Many of the spells, e.g. magic missile and fireball, 
also originate in D&D, although they are treated differently.  

The important thing though is to remember that nethack 
is not AD&D (NHINADD), so although things look similar, 
one cannot expect them to work as in AD&D.  

And back to playing nethack, AC can become as low as -127, 
but usually AC -40 is considered to suffice for ascension.  
A positive AC is extremely bad and should be lowered ASAP 
- unless that would make you burdened, that is.  

